\subsection{Why the Alignment Gap Narrows with Scale}
\label{sec:discuss_scaling}

The 355M results reveal that the alignment gap between RGDS and Random~50\% narrows from +72\% at 125M ($n{=}3$) to +18\% at 355M (seed=42), while the RGDS selection ratio at Generation~7 improves from $0.64 \pm 0.20$ to 0.83. Two mechanisms may explain this attenuation.

First, larger models maintain higher effective embedding dimensionality throughout self-training. GPT2-Medium's RankMe at Generation~7 (424.72 for RGDS) substantially exceeds GPT2-125M's ($292.54 \pm 5.91$), indicating that the representation space retains more discriminative dimensions even as content diversity degrades. A higher-dimensional embedding space is more resistant to the spectral collapse that drives proxy inversion: when more dimensions carry meaningful signal, the eigenvalue-based selection objective continues to track genuine content distinctions for longer.

Second, the 355M Uncurated baseline degrades far more severely (PPL~91.66 vs.\ $47.04 \pm 2.90$). Because the 50\%-budget methods train on half the data volume, their lower perplexity conflates any selection benefit with a data quantity effect---the comparison between 50\% and 100\% budgets is confounded. Nevertheless, the \emph{within-budget} comparison (RGDS vs.\ Random~50\%, both at 50\%) is unconfounded: both train on the same number of tokens, and the 18\% gap at 355M (vs.\ 72\% at 125M) directly measures the alignment gap's attenuation with scale.

These two effects---richer representations and a steeper uncurated collapse---both act to reduce the \emph{relative} impact of the alignment gap. However, the absolute penalty persists: RGDS remains 18\% worse than Random~50\% at 355M. The alignment gap does not vanish with scale; it is attenuated but not eliminated.%
